== Deities ==
The shrine enshrines the following four {{ill|Kami|ja|柱}} (deities), identical to those of {{ill|Kasuga Taisha|ja|春日大社}} in {{ill|Nara Prefecture|ja|奈良県}}:
* {{ill|Takemikazuchi|ja|武甕槌命}}
* {{ill|Izanagi-no-Mikoto|ja|斎主命}}
* {{ill|Amenokoyane no Mikoto|ja|天児屋根命}}
* {{ill|Hime no Kami|ja|比売神}}

== History ==
The shrine traces its origins to a {{ill|Kasuga Shrine|ja|春日祠}} enshrined at the Kasuga territory in {{ill|Tomi-da|ja|富田庄}} (modern {{ill|Irida|ja|入田町}}, Tokushima City), a former estate of Kasuga Shrine in {{ill|Yamato Province|ja|大和国}}. It is said to have been relocated to its current site at Otakiyama during the transfer of power to the {{ill|Hachisuka clan|ja|蜂須賀氏}} in 1586, coinciding with the construction of {{ill|Tokushima Castle|ja|徳島城}}. Another account suggests the shrine was moved from {{ill|Tamiya Village|ja|田宮村}} along with Shofuku-ji Temple, which later became the shrine’s {{ill|Bettōji|ja|別当寺}} before its abolition in the early {{ill|Meiji period|ja|明治時代}}.<ref>According to the ''Awa-shi'' (阿波志), compiled in 1815.</ref>

During the {{ill|Keicho era|ja|慶長}} (late 16th to early 17th century), the shrine was referred to as "Kasuga Daimyōjin" and received significant patronage from the Hachisuka clan, including an annual offering of 10 koku of rice. In 1873, it was officially renamed Kasuga Shrine and designated as a {{ill|Ken-sha|ja|県社}}. The shrine also features "Kasuga Spring" (春日水), one of the notable {{ill|Mount Bizan springs|ja|眉山湧水群}}.

